(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cardiomyopathy-vignettes"]={"title":"Cardiomyopathy Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with chest pain after a car accident that killed her sister has ST elevation and an elevated troponin; stress cardiomyopathy is suspected. Her pain has eased and she is hemodynamically stable in the emergency department. Which disposition is most appropriate?","choices":["Admit to cardiology","Discharge home today","Outpatient follow-up only","Refer for transplant listing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because Takotsubo mimics acute coronary syndrome with no reliable early differences, patients are admitted to cardiology and managed on acute coronary syndrome protocols with supportive treatment. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy comes to clinic with mild breathlessness on exertion. Her examination shows trace ankle edema and clear lungs. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 60 pg/mL. What does this natriuretic peptide result indicate?","choices":["Myocarditis is confirmed","Filling pressure is raised","Heart failure is excluded","Heart failure is confirmed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. B-type natriuretic peptide is released when ventricular filling pressures are high, and a level below 100 pg/mL excludes heart failure as the explanation for her current symptoms. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has stable dilated cardiomyopathy of two years' duration with no systemic illness and no recent change in his heart function. He has read about heart biopsies online and asks whether he should have one. What is the role of a heart muscle biopsy for him?","choices":["Not routine and rarely useful","Required to confirm every case","The gold standard for his disease","The first test before echocardiography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is not used routinely in dilated cardiomyopathy and is rarely useful. Its indications are a recent onset of rapidly deteriorating function or a systemic disease that can involve the heart. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has newly diagnosed hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with a septum of 2.2 cm and occasional palpitations. Her cardiologist wants to judge her risk of ventricular arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death. Which test assesses that risk?","choices":["A repeat chest radiograph","Ambulatory rhythm monitoring","Cardiac catheterization","A genetic testing panel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ambulatory electrocardiogram monitoring for 24 to 48 hours should be performed in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy to assess the risk of ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman is recovering in hospital from stress cardiomyopathy that followed news of her son's serious illness. Her chest pain has resolved, and she asks whether her heart will stay damaged. Which answer about her prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Poorest outlook of any type","Most patients recover completely","Half die within five years","It always progresses to failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Takotsubo is a transient regional systolic dysfunction, and most patients completely recover, which distinguishes its prognosis from the progressive cardiomyopathies. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man admitted with presumed viral myocarditis keeps deteriorating despite full medical treatment. Coronary angiography is normal, and valvular, toxic and ischemic causes of heart failure have been excluded. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Serum troponin","Endomyocardial biopsy","Cardiac catheterization","Chest radiograph"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Endomyocardial biopsy is the gold standard for myocarditis once other causes of heart failure are excluded, and it is recommended for acute deterioration of unknown origin that does not respond to treatment. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman received mediastinal radiation and chemotherapy for a chest cancer 15 years ago. She now has progressive exercise intolerance, orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat), ascites and swollen legs. Echocardiogram shows stiff ventricles of normal size, marked atrial enlargement and abnormal diastolic function with a preserved ejection fraction. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Acute myocarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mediastinal radiation and chemotherapy cause inflammation and then fibrosis that restricts filling, and a prior history of radiation or chemotherapy is a named clue in restrictive cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with acute myocarditis after a viral illness has fluid retention with bilateral leg edema and crackles, but his blood pressure is stable. He is receiving supportive care. Which medication may be added as needed for his fluid retention?","choices":["A thrombolytic drug","Alcohol septal ablation","A diuretic","A positive inotrope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Supportive care is the mainstay of myocarditis treatment, and medications are added as needed for the clinical scenario: diuretics for fluid retention, with vasodilators, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, antiarrhythmics and anticoagulation as indicated. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 23-year-old previously healthy man has breathlessness, chest pain and exercise intolerance ten days after a flulike illness with fever, myalgia and diarrhea. He is tachycardic with a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) and crackles at both bases. Troponin is elevated and coronary angiography is normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute viral myocarditis","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute heart failure in an otherwise healthy young man with no cardiac history, one to two weeks after a flulike prodrome, with raised troponin and normal coronaries, is acute viral myocarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy developed severe asthma symptoms when a beta blocker was started, and it was stopped. She still has exertional breathlessness from outflow obstruction. Which medication is the appropriate alternative?","choices":["Nifedipine, a vasodilator","Nitroglycerin paste","Norepinephrine infusion","Verapamil or diltiazem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers verapamil and diltiazem are the listed alternative to a beta blocker for outflow tract obstruction, slowing the heart and reducing contractility. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy without obstruction has brief runs of ventricular tachycardia on ambulatory monitoring. A defibrillator is being arranged. Which antiarrhythmic has been shown to reduce his risk of sudden cardiac death?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Epinephrine","Amiodarone","Digoxin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Amiodarone is proven to reduce the incidence and risk of sudden cardiac death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, with or without outflow obstruction, and it also converts and suppresses atrial fibrillation and flutter. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has crushing chest pain and breathlessness the day after her husband's funeral. She has no hypertension, diabetes or smoking history. The electrocardiogram shows ST elevation with T-wave inversion, troponin is elevated, and echocardiogram shows hypokinesis of the mid and apical segments of the left ventricle. Catheterization shows normal coronary arteries. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A postmenopausal woman after an emotional stressor, with ST elevation, raised troponin, mid and apical hypokinesis and normal coronary arteries at catheterization, has Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has months of progressive breathlessness, abdominal swelling and bilateral pedal edema. He prefers to sit upright. Jugular venous pressure is raised and he has a Kussmaul sign (jugular venous pressure rises with inspiration), hepatomegaly and ascites. His electrocardiogram shows low voltage (small complexes) in all leads, and echocardiogram shows normal-sized ventricles, marked biatrial enlargement and a preserved ejection fraction. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stiff ventricles with a near-normal cavity, preserved ejection fraction and big atria, presenting late with right-sided failure, are restrictive cardiomyopathy; low voltage points to amyloidosis, the most common cause in the United States. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy has breathlessness on exertion and peripheral edema. On examination her neck veins distend when firm pressure is held over her right upper abdomen, a sign called hepatojugular reflux (neck-vein distension with firm pressure over the liver). Which additional finding is expected with this degree of congestion?","choices":["Congestive enlargement of the liver","A small, soft, nonpalpable liver","A pulsatile mass in the epigastrium","Flank bruising from bleeding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The gastrointestinal findings of dilated cardiomyopathy are congestive hepatomegaly, hepatojugular reflux and ascites, all from venous congestion as the failing heart backs up blood. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure, ascites and a raised jugular venous pressure has cardiac magnetic resonance imaging to decide between restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis. Which imaging finding would point to constrictive pericarditis instead?","choices":["Marked atrial enlargement","Preserved ejection fraction","Normal ventricular cavity size","Pericardial thickening"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging is used to differentiate the two conditions, and pericardial thickening is the finding that identifies constrictive pericarditis rather than restrictive cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy comes to the emergency department with exertional chest pressure. His electrocardiogram shows left ventricular hypertrophy without acute changes, and a clinician reaches for a chest pain order set. Which medication should be avoided?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Metoprolol","Verapamil","Amiodarone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Drugs that reduce preload or afterload, such as nitrates, may increase symptoms by aggravating outflow tract obstruction, so nitroglycerin should be avoided if possible in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man is newly diagnosed with restrictive cardiomyopathy caused by hemochromatosis, with iron deposited in the heart tissue. He has congestion and fatigue. Which is the first principle of his management?","choices":["An inotrope to raise output","A high-intensity exercise program","Treat the underlying disorder","Septal myectomy for relief"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management of restrictive cardiomyopathy begins with treating the underlying disorder, such as iron storage in hemochromatosis, with cautious diuretics, rate-slowing drugs and anticoagulation added as needed. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"An 86-year-old woman with end-stage cardiomyopathy has breathlessness at rest, edema and fatigue despite maximal tolerated therapy. She is not a transplant or device candidate and says she wants comfort at home. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stop all of her symptom medicines","Add an assist device urgently","Discuss goals of care and hospice","Refer for heart transplant listing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Palliative care in cardiomyopathy covers symptom management of dyspnea, fatigue and edema, advance care planning and goals-of-care discussions, support for caregivers, and hospice referral in end-stage disease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy confirmed by echocardiography has typical findings and no plans for surgery or a device. A colleague suggests cardiac catheterization to complete the work-up. When is cardiac catheterization indicated in this condition?","choices":["Always, to rule out coronary disease","Only if the murmur is very loud","Only if surgery or a pacemaker is planned","Always, to confirm the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac catheterization is not required for the diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy; it is obtained when invasive therapy such as a pacemaker or surgery is planned. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has breathlessness on exertion and occasional palpitations. His father has hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. His pulse shows pulsus bisferiens (a double systolic peak), the apical impulse is forceful and sustained, and a fourth heart sound (a late diastolic sound just before the first heart sound) is present. Which is the typical diagnostic test of choice?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Echocardiography","Cardiac catheterization","Genetic testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography is the typical diagnostic test of choice for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, showing asymmetric septal hypertrophy, a small cavity, systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve and left atrial enlargement. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has progressive breathlessness on exertion, fatigue and swelling of both legs. The apical impulse is displaced laterally, a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) is present, and there is a soft murmur at the apex. Which echocardiogram finding would support dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Midsegment and apical hypokinesis alone","Dilated thin walls with a reduced ejection fraction","A thick septum and a small left ventricular cavity","Normal cavity size with large atria and stiff walls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dilated, thin walls are the prominent echocardiogram feature, with an ejection fraction below 40%, hypokinetic chambers, possibly all four chambers dilated, mitral or tricuspid regurgitation and sometimes thrombus. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man is newly diagnosed with dilated cardiomyopathy after a viral illness. He is in sinus rhythm, blood pressure is 118/72, and his ejection fraction is 30%. No thrombus is seen on echocardiogram. Which pair of drug classes should he receive whatever the cause of his disease?","choices":["Anticoagulant and antiarrhythmic started for everyone","Verapamil and a nitrate to relax the thick ventricle","A diuretic alone, until symptoms settle to baseline","Beta blocker and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of dilated cardiomyopathy is essentially that of chronic heart failure, and every patient, regardless of cause, should receive a beta blocker and an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, both of which reduce mortality. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and no high-risk features wants to stay active. He used to run marathons competitively and asks what level of exercise he should aim for. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Unrestricted competitive team sports","Strict bed rest for the rest of life","Heavy lifting to build heart strength","Mild to moderate noncompetitive activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy have historically been told to confine themselves to mild to moderate recreational activities in a noncompetitive manner, because sudden death relates to extreme exertion. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with stress cardiomyopathy confirmed by normal coronary arteries at catheterization is stable, with no ventricular thrombus and no signs of heart failure. Which medication is described as helpful in her supportive treatment?","choices":["An inotrope infusion","A thrombolytic drug","Amyl nitrite by inhalation","A beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of Takotsubo is empiric and supportive and depends on the presentation; beta blockers are described as helpful, with anticoagulation only for a thrombus and diuretics only for heart failure. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with restrictive cardiomyopathy is found to have cardiac amyloidosis, with amyloid protein deposited in the heart tissue. He has breathlessness, leg edema and ascites. Which electrocardiogram finding is expected with this infiltrative disease?","choices":["Left axis deviation","ST elevation","Low QRS voltage","Prominent Q waves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram in restrictive cardiomyopathy may show low QRS voltage in amyloidosis infiltrative disease, and atrial fibrillation and bundle branch blocks are also common. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy develops a new holosystolic murmur heard best at the apex that radiates to the left axilla. Her ventricle has continued to enlarge on serial echocardiograms. Which complication best accounts for the new murmur?","choices":["Pericardial thickening from constriction","Functional mitral regurgitation from dilation","Outflow obstruction from a thick septum","Tricuspid regurgitation into the axilla"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As the ventricle dilates, the valve apparatus is distorted, and mitral regurgitation (which appears earlier) and tricuspid regurgitation commonly develop. The mitral murmur is holosystolic at the apex, radiating to the axilla. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy has pulmonary and systemic congestion, with crackles and leg edema. A diuretic is being started. Which statement about diuretic use in her is most accurate?","choices":["Use with caution; stiff ventricles need preload","Contraindicated in any restrictive disease","Give aggressively to fully dry the lungs","Only useful once the ejection fraction falls"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics are used for pulmonary and systemic congestion in restrictive cardiomyopathy, but with caution, because they lower preload and the noncompliant ventricles depend on preload to maintain cardiac output. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman developed chest pain and dyspnea during a hurricane evacuation. She has ST elevation, an elevated troponin and B-type natriuretic peptide, and an echocardiogram showing akinesis of the left ventricular apex. Which test confirms stress cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Cardiac catheterization","Endomyocardial biopsy","Exercise stress test","Serial troponin levels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy is confirmed with cardiac catheterization, which most often shows normal coronary arteries despite the infarct-like presentation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy, sinus rhythm and an ejection fraction of 20% has a follow-up echocardiogram that shows a thrombus layered against the wall of the left ventricle. Which additional treatment is now indicated?","choices":["Stop the beta blocker","Alcohol septal ablation","Surgical myectomy now","Start anticoagulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dilated ventricles carry a risk of thromboembolic complications, and a known mural thrombus is one of the three indications for anticoagulation in dilated cardiomyopathy, with atrial fibrillation and an artificial valve. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has had an episode of fainting during basketball practice and brief runs of ventricular tachycardia on monitoring. She wants to keep playing on her competitive school team. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Activity restriction given her high risk","Unrestricted play with extra fluids","Clearance once the murmur softens","Competitive play on a beta blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Activity restriction for high-risk individuals is an adolescent strategy, and sudden death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is highest in preadolescents and adolescents and is related to extreme exertion. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"}]};
